Problem Statement
Schools and students often need tools to manage student information without the trouble or extra work of user-friendly screens. Keeping types of student profiles (like those who are studying for a bachelors degree versus those doing a masters in memory needs a good design with objects to avoid repeating code. Also data that is stored in memory must be saved to a file before the program ends to stop losing the information.
 Objectives
Object-Oriented Design: Create a base class called Student and a derived class called GradStudent. Use inheritance.
 Interface: Make a loop that lets users pick options while the program is running.
In-Memory State: Use Java Collections like Array List to store add and use student objects while the program is working.
Data Saving: Save the student information that's in memory to a file called student_records.txt when the program ends.
Fault Tolerant: Deal with input like letters when numbers are expected using try-catch blocks to stop the program from crashing.
 Functional Requirements
1: View All Records: The system must go through the list of students. Use a special display method to show each students information.
2: Register  Student: The system must ask for the students name and age make a Student object and add it to the list.
3: Export Data on Exit: The system must. Change a file called student_records.txt and write the names and ages of all the students in the list one per line when the user chooses option 3.
4: Terminate System: The system must end the loop safely after writing the data to the file.
Non-Functional Requirements
1: Usability:A simple and clean screen with instructions for the choices.
2: Fault Tolerance:The system must handle mistakes like entering letters of numbers without breaking.
3: Execution Speed: All the actions like adding to a list and writing to a file must happen fast.
4: Portability: The program must work on any computer that has Java Development Kit 8 or higher.
